> `[OWNER 2026-10-14, diff-1 F-x]`; readings both implementations chose independently are written in as rules tagged
> `[OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-xx/Axx]`; interface conventions are in section 3.5.

## What this document is for, and what it is not

This is the one place an implementer of the V1 verifier or issuer can read what V1 requires. It lists the
requirements, and each one points to where it was decided (source section and rule name; line numbers are
locators only, section 0). Together with A9 and ENCODING-V1 it is intended to be **sufficient with the frozen
catalog, the authenticated trust configuration and the pinned normative dependencies**; those external immutable
inputs do not disappear. [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F17, r2-14.2] *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: previously "each one
points to the line where it was decided"; that overstated it where a requirement points only to a later owner tag.)*

It is mostly an extraction. Where a rule is already decided, the source is tagged. Where it is not, the gap is
marked `OPEN` and says what must be decided. It also records a small number of **new** choices that no source makes;
each is labelled `OWNER PROFILE DECISION 2026-10-13`, is listed in section 0.1, and is not an A9 citation. It does not restate the byte encoding: for that, read
`formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md`. It does not hold operating numbers (window dates, stop margins, hardware): those are in
`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md` and are not profile rules. It is not the 2929-line spec, which stays as the design
reference and is synced after V1.

Where this document and a signed amendment disagree, the amendment wins.

## 0. How to read the tags

Every requirement ends with a tag `[source:line]`. Sources:

| Short | File |
|---|---|
| A9 | `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md` (signed by the author's commit `2ad3489`, 2026-10-16; `formal/p8/HYBRID-REGISTRATION-RECORD.md`) [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-12] *(Was: "(PROPOSED)".)* |
| A10 | `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md` (signed by the author's commit `9ade764`, 2026-10-18); cited by row number of its A10.7 table [OWNER 2026-10-19] |
| DD | `docs/V1-DERIVATION-DRAFT.md` |
| ENC | `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` (section numbers and line numbers) |
| SPEC | `docs/implementation-spec.md` |
| RB | `docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md` (reference only) |
| SR | `prototype/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md` (instrument evidence, never a rule by itself) |
| FT, GU | `prototype/FINDINGS-BY-TIER.md`, `prototype/GUESSES.md` (finding labels only) |
| RES | `docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/RESOLUTIONS-DRAFT.md` (owner-disposed 2026-10-14; rationale and conformance list §L) |

**Locators** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F17]. A9 section identifiers (A9.1 … A9.8) and stable rule names ("Root-KSK
rule", "Trust-configuration authority", "Git stamp target", C4, …) govern. The `A9:nnn` and `A10:nnn` line numbers
in this document were refreshed on 2026-10-24 against the signed texts (A9 at `2ad3489`, A10 at `9ade764`; table in
`docs/reviews/freeze-record/LOCATORS.md`) [OWNER 2026-10-24, FR-01]. They were earlier taken from a draft A9 layout
and were approximate (example: section 8's fallback bullet cited A9:338-340 for A9.6 "Signers"). Where a line number and a section or rule name disagree, the section and rule name govern. Every
`A9:nnn` locator is refreshed against the A9 **signing commit**, which the freeze record names together with the
freeze commit of every other source (section 14.2 item 7).
A tag `[OWNER 2026-10-14, Gxx]` marks a rule decided by the owner's 2026-10-14 disposition of spec-sufficiency gap
Gxx (RES). Its rationale, label (CLARIFY, OWNER CHOICE (supported-path limit), BROADEN, NARROW-CLAIM, CORRECT) and
conformance cases are in RES under that gap. These are owner profile decisions in the sense of section 0.1, except
   372	- **Registered fallback, otherwise:** both slots classical P-256 under P4': KMS-backed OpenPGP P-256, plus the
   373	  `gpg` slot under slot-2 custody. The signature-inclusive anchor and the
   374	  issuance window are retained unchanged.
   375	- **Slot-2 custody (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13).** The `gpg`-slot key is generated non-exportable in hardware outside
   376	  the issuing AWS account: a token on author-controlled hardware, or an HSM-backed key at a provider other than AWS.
   377	  The owner chooses between them, and records the choice, dated, in the profile before the 30 Oct freeze. The choice
   378	  takes into account measured token throughput and issuance availability, and the author's concern about scalability
   379	  (2026-10-13: *"Scalability is a real, legitimate, concern"*). Either choice satisfies the author's key-protection
   380	  requirement (A9.0) and leaves C1–C7 unchanged. The key's authority is evidenced by the key-publication record in
   381	  both cases.
   382	  - *Policy signer.* In either custody option, a queue-pulling policy signer outside the issuing AWS account
   383	    validates the complete V1 frame and enforces the declared-time check using its own clock before requesting a
   384	    signature. The issuing AWS account can enqueue frames but cannot invoke the slot-2 key directly, change that
   385	    policy or clock, or obtain the policy signer's key-use credentials. The slot-2 key is dedicated to V1 tagged
   386	    type-0x00 documents (attestation and manifest-possession). The dated custody record names this enforcement
   387	    boundary and its access policy. Non-exportability protects against key export, not signing misuse through the
   388	    permitted queue path.
   389	  - *Deadline.* For the hybrid, the final custody implementation must have completed the deployed-slot
   390	    pass-condition evidence by 21 Oct. The owner may defer selection until before 30 Oct only among custody
   391	    implementations that each met that deadline; otherwise the registered fallback is taken. A custody selection
   392	    changes neither rule O nor any claim, pass condition or exit obligation.
   393	  - *Limit.* Separate technical custody does not imply independent availability, independent signing fates,
   394	    separate control or independent approval of content; C3 applies to both options.
    40	
    41	The second revision let any worker latch or expire, and relied on compare-and-set plus re-observation. Codex found
    42	interleavings in which a stale or partial fetch expires an attempt that another, stalled worker had already observed
    43	on time (ILC-01). Compare-and-set makes the writes exclusive. It does not make the earlier observation win. Revision 3
    44	removes the concurrency instead of arbitrating it.
    45	
    46	1. **One writer.** Every lifecycle transition (start an attempt, latch, discard, expire, refuse, complete) is made
    47	   only by the poller, which runs with reserved concurrency 1 (`service/issuer/infra/main.tf:354`). The API is
    48	   read-only for the lifecycle: `api.status` no longer calls `settle` (`api.py:194`). Every transition is a write
    49	   guarded on the expected state *and* attempt id, so a write meant for an ended attempt fails (ILC-05).
    50	2. **Attempt isolation.** As before: each attempt's declared time, frames, signature jobs, proofs and evidence live
    51	   under its id. A late response names its attempt and can never fill another.
    52	3. **Latch.** On each poll, for each open attempt, the poller fetches and upgrades the attempt's evidence and
    53	   evaluates the three-conjunct predicate with k-deep burial (see "What is registered"). If it holds, the poller
    54	   writes the latch with `observed_at` (a wall reading taken after the evidence is in hand) and the digests of the
    55	   qualifying presentation, which is kept. The observation counts once that write is durable. A latch may be written
    56	   at any time before the attempt expires: the A2.1 "may" is exercised permissively.
    57	4. **Expiry.** An attempt expires only when all of these hold:
    58	   - (a) it is not latched, as read by the poller in this invocation;
    59	   - (b) the wall reading, taken after that read, is past `B + WRITE_LEASE_S`. B itself is never moved earlier;
    60	     waiting the lease longer only lets any latch write from a killed earlier invocation (begun with
    61	     `observed_at ≤ B`) commit first, under the stated write-duration assumption
    62	     (`docs/decisions/2026-10-27-service-clock-tolerance.md`);
    63	   - (c) this invocation's own fresh fetch, from every calendar the attempt was stamped to and the configured
    64	     explorers, does not establish eligibility.
    65	
    66	   Expiry is permitted strictly after B, so expiring later than B is allowed. What remains is a stated residual: an
    67	   on-time observation that was never made durable (its invocation was killed before the write) is lost if the
    68	   evidence sources then regress, for example during a calendar or explorer outage. Evidence otherwise only

